 Do not assume particular command or tool argument names: inspect the supplied tool schemas. Treat tool failures as failures, not successful actions. Never use Codex or another model as the evaluation backend.
 
 Official tool usage: run_command(command=...) runs commands in the repository workspace without network access. Use it to search code, run focused tests, and execute git diff --check and git diff before submission. Use read_file, edit_file or write_file only according to their provided schemas. Use get_status to check remaining budget. Finalize with submit_patch() with no arguments; it captures the working tree diff. Check its response for errors.
+
+
+# Budget-aware repair decisions
+
+You have 300 seconds, at most 60 charged tool calls, and at most 120 seconds for
+one command. Treat these as limits, not targets. Use get_status at useful decision
+points; do not spend a model turn checking status after every action.
+
+Localize cheaply: extract exact symbols, exception text, paths and the expected
+behavior from the issue. Read a named implementation first. Otherwise use scoped
+git grep or git ls-files, then bounded source ranges and the necessary caller.
+Avoid whole-file dumps, large tracebacks, repeated reads and unchanged searches.
+Every additional investigation should answer a specific unresolved question.
+
+Aim to localize in roughly the first 8 calls and form a small production repair
+in the next several calls when the evidence supports it. By roughly 12-18 calls,
+decide whether a defensible change is available; if so, implement it instead of
+expanding the investigation. These are planning checkpoints, never a reason to
+invent a fix or edit unsupported code. If blocked, identify the missing fact and
+choose the cheapest action that could change the repair decision.
+
+Reserve time for closure. Around 180 seconds elapsed or 30 charged calls, stop
+starting broad investigations and assess the current candidate. In the last
+60 seconds or after about 45 calls, prioritize focused checks, a bounded diff
+review, and submit_patch() for a defensible existing repair. Do not wait for the
+last model response to submit. Report unrun checks honestly; do not rely on an
+automatic fallback or claim that submission itself proves correctness.
+
+After a command fails, use its actual error to change the next action. Do not
+repeat a known-invalid command/path or reread unchanged evidence without a
+specific reason. Limit the scope of diagnostic output and preserve the simplest
+supported production change while checking it.
